# CODEOWNERS -- who must approve a change to each path.
#
# This file is the mechanism behind spec section 3: authorship is separated
# from ownership. A consumer team authors a contract change as a pull request
# against a contract package it does not own; the provider team that owns that
# package approves it. Nobody waits on backend capacity to get a contract
# written, and nobody merges a contract the provider has not seen.
#
#
# THE HANDLES BELOW ARE PLACEHOLDERS
#
# `@marcos-corp/team-a` and `@marcos-corp/team-b` are written in GitHub's
# org/team form to match the `@marcos-corp/*` package scope, but they resolve
# to nothing on this repository, which is owned by a user account rather than
# an organisation. GitHub does not fail a push, a pull request, or a check on
# an unresolvable owner: the rule simply matches no reviewer, and the approval
# requirement disappears with no warning anywhere. Replace both handles with
# real users or teams before treating any of this as enforced -- that swap is
# tracked in `.plans/PREREQUISITES-01-bare-minimal-api-governance-poc.md`.
#
#
# HOW THESE RULES ARE READ
#
# - Last match wins. Unlike .gitignore, ownership does not accumulate across
#   matching lines: the final matching rule for a path is the only one that
#   applies. The blocks below do not overlap, so no rule here is shadowed --
#   keep it that way, or list the more general pattern first.
# - A leading `/` anchors the pattern to the repository root. Without it,
#   `packages/db/` would also match a `packages/db/` nested anywhere deeper in
#   the tree.
# - Two owners on one line means either one can satisfy the requirement for
#   those paths, not that both must approve. A pull request that touches a
#   team-a-only path AND a team-b-only path needs one approval from each,
#   because each path's own owner set has to be satisfied.
# - Paths not listed here have no owner. CODEOWNERS only ever adds a review
#   requirement; it never removes the repository's default one.
# - None of this is binding until branch protection on `main` requires review
#   from code owners. Until then these lines request reviewers and document
#   intent, which is not the same as a gate.


# --- Team A ------------------------------------------------------------------

# Provider. Owns the contract that web-a, web-b and service-b all consume, so
# this is the path where cross-team pull requests land for approval.
/packages/contracts-service-a/   @marcos-corp/team-a
/services/service-a/             @marcos-corp/team-a

# Consumer, same team as its provider. The "no ceremony needed" case: a change
# spanning web-a and service-a needs one team's approval, not two.
/apps/web-a/                     @marcos-corp/team-a


# --- Team B ------------------------------------------------------------------

# Provider of invoices, and consumer of service-a. Ownership shifts by path,
# not by team: team B owns these, and opens pull requests against
# `packages/contracts-service-a/` like any other consumer.
/packages/contracts-service-b/   @marcos-corp/team-b
/services/service-b/             @marcos-corp/team-b

# Consumer across a team boundary. web-b needing a field from service-a is the
# governance case this whole repository exists to demonstrate.
/apps/web-b/                     @marcos-corp/team-b


# --- Shared between both teams -----------------------------------------------

# The database schema is owned by the service teams jointly and is explicitly
# not a dependency of any contract package (spec section 3). Joint ownership
# here is deliberate: a column rename must be visible to both teams, because
# the mapping layer that absorbs it lives in each service.
/packages/db/                    @marcos-corp/team-a @marcos-corp/team-b

# The shared CI machinery -- house ruleset, emit, diff, mock, dependency and
# pin checks. Whoever changes a gate changes it for both teams, so both own it.
/packages/contract-tooling/      @marcos-corp/team-a @marcos-corp/team-b
